// A feature's control state is an XState machine (docs/contributing/clients-architecture.md,
// "State"): a delay is an `after` transition and cancellation is leaving the state that owns the
// work. Hand-rolled timers and abort controllers in `state/` and `effects/` are the bookkeeping
// that kept producing stale replies and orphaned timers, so they are not written there. Any
// reference counts, not only a call: `const later = setTimeout` schedules all the same.
// The modules `biome.json` excludes from this rule await their machines, a list that only
// shrinks; biome.json takes no comments, so the list is tracked here and in #1969.
language js;

or {
  JsIdentifierExpression(name = `setTimeout`),
  JsIdentifierExpression(name = `setInterval`),
  JsIdentifierExpression(name = `AbortController`),
  `globalThis.setTimeout`,
  `globalThis.setInterval`,
  `globalThis.AbortController`
} as $reference where {
  register_diagnostic(
    span = $reference,
    message = "A feature's state/ and effects/ do not schedule timers or abort work by hand. Use an `after` transition for a delay and leave the state to cancel an invoked operation.",
    severity = "error"
  )
}
